-- title:
--   Zero-scheme ideal of c Ω on affine opens inside a frame
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and $N$ a module over the structure sheaf of $X$ which is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ for which the pullback of $N$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit module of $U$. Let $V$ be an open of $X$ and $\Omega \in \Gamma(N, V)$ a frame of $N$ on $V$, i.e. for every open $W \le V$ the map $\Gamma(X, W) \to \Gamma(N, W)$, $g \mapsto g \cdot (\Omega|_W)$, is bijective. Let $\sigma$ be a morphism from the unit module to $N$, and $c \in \Gamma(X, V)$ a function such that the section $\sigma$ attaches to the unit section $1 \in \Gamma(X, V)$ equals $c \cdot \Omega$. Then for every affine open $U$ of $X$ with $U \le V$, the value at $U$ of the ideal sheaf datum `Scheme.Modules.zeroSchemeIdeal σ` — the infimum of all ideal sheaf data $J$ on $X$ satisfying, for every affine open $W$, that the ideal generated by the range of the coefficient map `Scheme.Modules.coeff σ W` is contained in $J$ at $W$ — is the principal ideal of $\Gamma(X, U)$ generated by the restriction of $c$ to $U$.
--
--   This is the local description of the zero scheme of a section of an invertible module: on an affine open contained in a trivialising open, the zero-scheme ideal is cut out by the single coordinate of the section with respect to a frame. It is the computational form of the zero-scheme construction used in the relative Picard and Néron-model material, for instance in the recognition of unit modules at nodes and in the projective presentation of polarised abelian schemes.
-- source:
